WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.813
Lesson 6 works best when the learner sees one common magnetic-force idea instead of two disconnected tricks.

2
00:00:04.813 --> 00:00:09.625
Particle case means path bends.

3
00:00:09.625 --> 00:00:14.438
A sideways magnetic force can keep changing the direction of motion and create a curved path.

4
00:00:14.438 --> 00:00:19.251
Motor case means coil turns.

5
00:00:19.251 --> 00:00:24.063
Equal opposite forces on different sides of a current loop create a turning couple.

6
00:00:24.063 --> 00:00:28.876
Shared idea means perpendicular magnetic force.

7
00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:33.689
The same geometry is driving both stories.

8
00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:38.501
The weak move is to memorize circular motion in one box and motor effect in another, without noticing the common force idea underneath.

9
00:00:38.501 --> 00:00:43.314
The worked example becomes easier because the learner is translating one magnetic-force picture into two applications.

10
00:00:43.314 --> 00:00:48.127
One sideways-force idea explains both orbit and turning.

11
00:00:48.127 --> 00:00:52.939
Keep this takeaway in view: Treat the lesson as one geometry story in two contexts.

12
00:00:52.939 --> 00:00:57.752
Particle case means path bends.

13
00:00:57.752 --> 00:01:02.565
Magnetic force steers a moving charge.

14
00:01:02.565 --> 00:01:07.377
Motor case means coil turns.

15
00:01:07.377 --> 00:01:12.190
Opposite forces can create a couple.

16
00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.557
This diagram matters because it shows that the lesson is not changing topic completely.

17
00:01:16.557 --> 00:01:20.924
It is reusing the same magnetic-force geometry in two different systems.

18
00:01:20.924 --> 00:01:25.291
Orbit side means continuous turning.

19
00:01:25.291 --> 00:01:29.658
A moving charge keeps feeling a sideways force, so the path keeps bending.

20
00:01:29.658 --> 00:01:34.024
Motor side means turning couple.

21
00:01:34.024 --> 00:01:38.391
Opposite sides of a loop feel opposite forces at different positions.

22
00:01:38.391 --> 00:01:42.758
Lesson gain means one geometry, two outcomes.

23
00:01:42.758 --> 00:01:47.125
That connection is the real conceptual win of the lesson.

24
00:01:47.125 --> 00:01:51.492
Without this bridge, the learner may think circular motion and motor turning need unrelated explanations.

25
00:01:51.492 --> 00:01:55.859
The worked example becomes easier because the same force language can be reused cleanly.

26
00:01:55.859 --> 00:02:00.226
Read both contexts through the same magnetic-force lens.

27
00:02:00.226 --> 00:02:04.592
Keep this takeaway in view: The diagram is a bridge, not a topic split.

28
00:02:04.592 --> 00:02:08.959
Orbit side means continuous turning.

29
00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:13.326
The force keeps steering the particle.

30
00:02:13.326 --> 00:02:17.693
Motor side means turning couple.

31
00:02:17.693 --> 00:02:22.060
Separated forces create rotation.

32
00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:26.667
A sideways-push analogy helps because the outcome depends on what structure is being pushed.

33
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:31.274
One moving particle curves, while a two-sided loop can turn because the forces act at different positions.

34
00:02:31.274 --> 00:02:35.881
Single moving object means curved path.

35
00:02:35.881 --> 00:02:40.488
A repeated sideways push changes direction again and again.

36
00:02:40.488 --> 00:02:45.094
Extended loop means turning effect.

37
00:02:45.094 --> 00:02:49.701
Separated forces create rotation instead of simple translation.

38
00:02:49.701 --> 00:02:54.308
Lesson gain means same force, new structure.

39
00:02:54.308 --> 00:02:58.915
The geometry is shared even though the visible outcome changes.

40
00:02:58.915 --> 00:03:03.522
The weak analogy mistake is to focus only on motion size and forget that position of the force matters in the motor case.

41
00:03:03.522 --> 00:03:08.129
The worked example becomes clearer because the learner now expects a couple from separated forces.

42
00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:12.736
Outcome depends on structure, but the force geometry is shared.

43
00:03:12.736 --> 00:03:17.342
Keep this takeaway in view: Common force, different structure, different visible outcome.

44
00:03:17.342 --> 00:03:21.949
Single moving object means curved path.

45
00:03:21.949 --> 00:03:26.556
Repeated side-kicks bend the motion.

46
00:03:26.556 --> 00:03:31.163
Extended loop means turning effect.

47
00:03:31.163 --> 00:03:35.770
Separated side-kicks twist the loop.

48
00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:41.071
The formula board becomes much stronger when orbit motion is read as magnetic force becoming centripetal force, while motor motion is read as separated magnetic forces creating a couple.

49
00:03:41.071 --> 00:03:46.372
Orbit route means force balance.

50
00:03:46.372 --> 00:03:51.673
Set magnetic force equal to centripetal force for circular motion.

51
00:03:51.673 --> 00:03:56.974
Radius reading means bigger m or v means bigger r.

52
00:03:56.974 --> 00:04:02.276
Bigger q or B means tighter curvature.

53
00:04:02.276 --> 00:04:07.577
Motor route means forces at different positions.

54
00:04:07.577 --> 00:04:12.878
That positional separation is why the loop turns.

55
00:04:12.878 --> 00:04:18.179
Keep these relations visible: qvB = mv^2 / r; r = mv / (qB); and opposite forces on different coil sides form a turning couple.

56
00:04:18.179 --> 00:04:23.480
The particle orbit comes from force balance.

57
00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.781
The motor effect comes from equal opposite forces acting at different positions on the loop.

58
00:04:28.781 --> 00:04:34.082
A common error is to think the magnetic force changes the particle speed in the orbit case, or to think opposite forces on the coil simply cancel with no turning effect.

59
00:04:34.082 --> 00:04:39.383
The worked example becomes more coherent because orbit and motor language now feel connected.

60
00:04:39.383 --> 00:04:44.684
Use magnetic-force balance for the orbit and separated forces for the couple.

61
00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:49.986
Keep this takeaway in view: Both sides of the lesson are still magnetic-force stories.

62
00:04:49.986 --> 00:04:55.287
Orbit route means force balance.

63
00:04:55.287 --> 00:05:00.588
Magnetic force supplies the centripetal force.

64
00:05:00.588 --> 00:05:05.889
Radius reading means bigger m or v means bigger r.

65
00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:11.190
Bigger q or B means tighter curve.

66
00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:15.393
A dependable Lesson 6 routine begins by asking which structure the force is acting on.

67
00:05:15.393 --> 00:05:19.596
If it is a single moving charge, follow the orbit branch.

68
00:05:19.596 --> 00:05:23.799
If it is a loop, follow the turning-couple branch.

69
00:05:23.799 --> 00:05:28.002
Branch 1 means single moving charge.

70
00:05:28.002 --> 00:05:32.205
Use magnetic force as the centripetal force and read the radius.

71
00:05:32.205 --> 00:05:36.408
Branch 2 means current loop.

72
00:05:36.408 --> 00:05:40.611
Look for equal opposite forces acting on different sides.

73
00:05:40.611 --> 00:05:44.814
Shared start means sideways magnetic force.

74
00:05:44.814 --> 00:05:49.016
Both branches still begin from the same field geometry.

75
00:05:49.016 --> 00:05:53.219
If the branch is not chosen first, learners can try to use the orbit formula in the motor context or forget the force-balance logic in the particle context.

76
00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:57.422
The worked example becomes easier because the route is chosen before the algebra begins.

77
00:05:57.422 --> 00:06:01.625
Choose the structure first, then apply the right branch.

78
00:06:01.625 --> 00:06:05.828
Keep this takeaway in view: One force idea, two structured branches.

79
00:06:05.828 --> 00:06:10.031
Branch 1 means single moving charge.

80
00:06:10.031 --> 00:06:14.234
Use the orbit route.

81
00:06:14.234 --> 00:06:18.437
Branch 2 means current loop.

82
00:06:18.437 --> 00:06:22.640
Use the turning-couple route.

83
00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:27.422
The radius relation is powerful because it immediately shows what makes the path wider or tighter.

84
00:06:27.422 --> 00:06:32.205
A heavier or faster particle bends less sharply, while a stronger field or larger charge bends it more tightly.

85
00:06:32.205 --> 00:06:36.988
Bigger m means bigger radius.

86
00:06:36.988 --> 00:06:41.770
More inertia means harder turning.

87
00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:46.552
Bigger v means bigger radius.

88
00:06:46.552 --> 00:06:51.335
Faster motion needs a wider circle for the same sideways force.

89
00:06:51.335 --> 00:06:56.118
Bigger q or B means smaller radius.

90
00:06:56.118 --> 00:07:00.900
A stronger magnetic effect bends the path more tightly.

91
00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:05.682
The weak move is to memorize the rearranged formula without extracting the direction of each proportional effect.

92
00:07:05.682 --> 00:07:10.465
The worked example becomes easier because the final radius can be checked for reasonableness before the calculator confirms it.

93
00:07:10.465 --> 00:07:15.248
Use the formula as a proportional map, not just as arithmetic.

94
00:07:15.248 --> 00:07:20.030
Keep this takeaway in view: Radius reasoning should feel qualitative before it feels numerical.

95
00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:24.812
Bigger m means bigger radius.

96
00:07:24.812 --> 00:07:29.595
Heavier particles resist turning more.

97
00:07:29.595 --> 00:07:34.378
Bigger v means bigger radius.

98
00:07:34.378 --> 00:07:39.160
Faster motion widens the orbit.

99
00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:43.028
In the orbit case, the magnetic force stays perpendicular to the motion.

100
00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:46.895
That means it is excellent at redirecting the velocity but not at acting like a forward driving force.

101
00:07:46.895 --> 00:07:50.762
Perpendicular force means changes direction.

102
00:07:50.762 --> 00:07:54.630
The velocity keeps turning.

103
00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:58.497
Circular result means repeated redirection.

104
00:07:58.497 --> 00:08:02.365
A continuous sideways force sustains circular motion.

105
00:08:02.365 --> 00:08:06.232
Lesson gain means orbit explanation.

106
00:08:06.232 --> 00:08:10.100
Curving the path does not require a forward shove.

107
00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.968
A weak explanation says the field keeps making the particle go faster around the circle, but the key role is redirection.

108
00:08:13.968 --> 00:08:17.835
This makes the worked example more physically meaningful when orbit radius is calculated.

109
00:08:17.835 --> 00:08:21.702
Orbit motion is a redirection story first.

110
00:08:21.702 --> 00:08:25.570
Keep this takeaway in view: Steering is enough to make a circle.

111
00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:29.438
Perpendicular force means changes direction.

112
00:08:29.438 --> 00:08:33.305
The force keeps steering.

113
00:08:33.305 --> 00:08:37.172
Circular result means repeated redirection.

114
00:08:37.172 --> 00:08:41.040
That sustains the bend.

115
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.450
The motor side of the lesson becomes clearer when the learner stops asking only whether the forces are equal and starts asking where those forces act on the loop.

116
00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:49.860
Equal forces means same size.

117
00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:54.270
The two active sides can feel equal force magnitudes.

118
00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:58.680
Different positions means twisting effect.

119
00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:03.090
Because the forces act on different sides, they create rotation.

120
00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:07.500
Lesson gain means couple language.

121
00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.910
Cancellation of net force does not remove the turning effect.

122
00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:16.320
The weak statement says the forces cancel so nothing happens, but that ignores their separated points of action.

123
00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.730
The worked example becomes easier because the learner now expects turning even from equal opposite forces.

124
00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:25.140
Separated equal opposite forces can create rotation.

125
00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:29.550
Keep this takeaway in view: Ask where the forces act, not only how big they are.

126
00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:33.960
Equal forces means same size.

127
00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:38.370
Magnitude alone is not the whole story.

128
00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:42.780
Different positions means twisting effect.

129
00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:47.190
Position creates the couple.

130
00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:51.321
For a single particle, a perpendicular magnetic force keeps bending the path.

131
00:09:51.321 --> 00:09:55.452
For a loop, the same perpendicular-force logic acts on separated sides and produces a turn.

132
00:09:55.452 --> 00:09:59.584
The structure changes the visible outcome, but not the core idea.

133
00:09:59.584 --> 00:10:03.715
Single particle means curved trajectory.

134
00:10:03.715 --> 00:10:07.846
One object keeps being redirected.

135
00:10:07.846 --> 00:10:11.977
Current loop means turning effect.

136
00:10:11.977 --> 00:10:16.108
Two sides receive separated magnetic pushes.

137
00:10:16.108 --> 00:10:20.239
Unified lens means same force question.

138
00:10:20.239 --> 00:10:24.371
Ask what a sideways magnetic force does to the structure in front of you.

139
00:10:24.371 --> 00:10:28.502
The weak move is to memorize two finished answers and miss the common generative question underneath them.

140
00:10:28.502 --> 00:10:32.633
That gives the worked example a stronger conceptual frame before the numbers arrive.

141
00:10:32.633 --> 00:10:36.764
Use one force question to unify both contexts.

142
00:10:36.764 --> 00:10:40.895
Keep this takeaway in view: One magnetic-force lens unifies the lesson.

143
00:10:40.895 --> 00:10:45.026
Single particle means curved trajectory.

144
00:10:45.026 --> 00:10:49.158
Repeated redirection bends the path.

145
00:10:49.158 --> 00:10:53.289
Current loop means turning effect.

146
00:10:53.289 --> 00:10:57.420
Separated forces twist the loop.

147
00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:01.024
Use this checkpoint to test whether the lesson has unified properly.

148
00:11:01.024 --> 00:11:04.629
Both contexts are consequences of a perpendicular magnetic force.

149
00:11:04.629 --> 00:11:08.233
In one case it keeps redirecting a moving charge.

150
00:11:08.233 --> 00:11:11.838
In the other it acts on different coil sides and forms a turning couple.

151
00:11:11.838 --> 00:11:15.442
Question means shared idea.

152
00:11:15.442 --> 00:11:19.046
What single force idea explains both contexts?

153
00:11:19.046 --> 00:11:22.651
Route means perpendicular magnetic force.

154
00:11:22.651 --> 00:11:26.255
Start from the same sideways-force geometry.

155
00:11:26.255 --> 00:11:29.859
Answer check means structure changes outcome.

156
00:11:29.859 --> 00:11:33.464
Particle gives a curved path and coil gives a turn.

157
00:11:33.464 --> 00:11:37.068
One sideways magnetic-force idea drives both outcomes.

158
00:11:37.068 --> 00:11:40.672
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep the unifying idea visible right into the example.

159
00:11:40.672 --> 00:11:44.277
Question means shared idea.

160
00:11:44.277 --> 00:11:47.881
Look for one force principle, not two separate tricks.

161
00:11:47.881 --> 00:11:51.486
Route means perpendicular magnetic force.

162
00:11:51.486 --> 00:11:55.090
This is the common core.

163
00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:59.624
Carry one bridge into the worked example.

164
00:11:59.624 --> 00:12:04.158
Start with perpendicular magnetic force.

165
00:12:04.158 --> 00:12:08.693
If the question is a moving charge, use magnetic force as the centripetal force and solve for radius.

166
00:12:08.693 --> 00:12:13.227
If the question is a loop, track the separated forces that create the turning couple.

167
00:12:13.227 --> 00:12:17.761
Step 1 means shared force.

168
00:12:17.761 --> 00:12:22.295
Start from perpendicular magnetic force.

169
00:12:22.295 --> 00:12:26.829
Step 2 means particle or coil?.

170
00:12:26.829 --> 00:12:31.364
Choose the correct structural branch.

171
00:12:31.364 --> 00:12:35.898
Step 3 means follow the branch.

172
00:12:35.898 --> 00:12:40.432
Orbit means force balance.

173
00:12:40.432 --> 00:12:44.966
Coil means turning couple.

174
00:12:44.966 --> 00:12:49.501
Keep these relations visible: particle branch: qvB = mv^2 / r and r = mv / (qB); coil branch: opposite forces on different sides create a turning couple; and both branches start from perpendicular magnetic force.

175
00:12:49.501 --> 00:12:54.035
Choose the structure first, then follow the correct branch while keeping the same sideways-force idea in view.

176
00:12:54.035 --> 00:12:58.569
One shared force idea should survive into either example branch.

177
00:12:58.569 --> 00:13:03.103
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep the bridge visible even after the branch is chosen.

178
00:13:03.103 --> 00:13:07.637
Step 1 means shared force.

179
00:13:07.637 --> 00:13:12.172
Start from the same magnetic-force geometry.

180
00:13:12.172 --> 00:13:16.706
Step 2 means particle or coil?.

181
00:13:16.706 --> 00:13:21.240
Choose the structure before the algebra.
